                "Econometric MMM accounts for two foundational non-linear marketing phenomena: carryover effects (adstock) and diminishing marginal returns (saturation). Advertising exposure rarely produces immediate, ephemeral consumer action; rather, brand advertising builds mental availability that decays gradually over subsequent weeks or months. Mathematically, media carryover is modeled through geometric decay or flexible two-parameter Weibull adstock transformations. Under geometric decay, the adstocked media expenditure A_{t,m} for channel m at time t is formalized as A_{t,m} = X_{t,m} + theta_m * A_{t-1,m}, where X_{t,m} represents gross spend at time t and theta_m in [0, 1) represents the retention rate. For television and brand sponsorships exhibiting delayed peak impacts, Weibull adstock models separate the delay parameter (scale) from the decay rate (shape), capturing complex S-curve awareness build-up that simple geometric models fail to represent.",
                "Concurrently, marketing channels exhibit diminishing marginal returns governed by psychological saturation: as ad exposure frequency intensifies, incremental audience reach plateaus and marginal consumer responsiveness declines toward zero. Modern econometric architectures model saturation through the Hill transformation function: S(A_{t,m}; K_m, S_m) = (A_{t,m}^{S_m}) / (K_m^{S_m} + A_{t,m}^{S_m}), where K_m represents the half-saturation point (the expenditure level achieving 50% of maximum potential sales response) and S_m represents the Hill slope parameter governing curve steepness. When S_m > 1, the response function exhibits an initial S-curve threshold before entering diminishing returns; when S_m <= 1, the curve exhibits purely concave diminishing returns from the first dollar invested.",
