                "The ultimate objective of econometric media mix modeling is not merely historical diagnostic reporting, but prescriptive capital allocation: determining the exact, profit-maximizing distribution of corporate marketing spend across all channels, brands, and geographic territories. When enterprise marketing budgets scale into tens or hundreds of millions of dollars, relying on historical budget inertia, executive intuition, or average ROAS rankings results in catastrophic capital waste. In accordance with microeconomic optimization theory, total corporate revenue is maximized across an investment portfolio not when average ROAS is maximized, but when marginal ROAS (mROAS) is equalized across all active channels.",
                "To understand marginal optimization, consider two marketing channels: Channel A (e.g., Brand Search) exhibiting a high average ROAS of 6.0x but severe saturation (steep diminishing returns), and Channel B (e.g., Streaming TV) exhibiting a moderate average ROAS of 2.8x but shallow saturation. If a marketing manager allocates an additional $1M based on average ROAS, they will pour the capital into Channel A, where the saturated marginal return on the next dollar is only $0.40 (a net economic loss). Conversely, allocating the dollar to Channel B yields a marginal return of $2.20. Under microeconomic equimarginal principles, capital must be continuously reallocated from low-mROAS channels to high-mROAS channels until the marginal return per dollar invested is identical across all channels: mROAS_1 = mROAS_2 = ... = mROAS_M = lambda.",
